# Tauri's build script (tauri-build -> tauri_utils::acl::build::
# autogenerate_command_permissions) regenerates these permission manifests on
# every `cargo check`/`cargo build` via `write_if_changed`, which does a raw
# byte comparison against freshly generated content and rewrites the file on
# any mismatch. The generated content always uses LF.
#
# Without this override, `core.autocrlf=true` (this project's Windows
# baseline, see CLAUDE.md) checks these files out with CRLF. The very first
# build then finds the on-disk CRLF bytes don't match the LF it generates,
# rewrites the file back to LF (byte-identical to the committed blob), and
# `git status` reports it modified anyway — because those LF bytes no longer
# match what a CRLF checkout would have produced. Every worktree ends up
# permanently dirty across all 130+ autogenerated files (#472).
#
# Pinning `eol=lf` makes checkout itself produce LF, matching what the build
# script writes, so there is nothing left for a build to "fix" and nothing
# left for git to flag.
#
# These files are committed on purpose, despite each one's own "DO NOT EDIT!"
# header nudging toward "just regenerate it, why is this in git" — every
# build regenerates them before `generate_context!` runs, so committing them
# is NOT what makes `tests/acl_manifest.rs` pass. The reason to keep them
# committed is diff visibility: they are security-relevant ACL grants, and a
# real change to one (e.g. #460 adding record_copilot_launch_posture) needs
# to show up as a reviewable diff, not vanish into build output. Don't "fix"
# the DO-NOT-EDIT tension by deleting them from git.
src-tauri/permissions/autogenerated/*.toml text eol=lf

# Vendored impeccable skill (see .claude/skills/impeccable/README.md and
# THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md). Upstream ships these files LF, and the vendoring
# contract is byte-identity with upstream: `do not hand-edit, re-vendor to
# update` only means anything if a reviewer can diff our copy against the
# upstream blob and get silence. Under `core.autocrlf=true` a checkout would
# rewrite all 154 of them to CRLF, so every such diff would report every file
# as changed and the pristine-vendor check would be unrunnable. Pinning eol=lf
# makes the worktree copy match the upstream bytes directly.
.claude/skills/impeccable/** text eol=lf
.claude/agents/impeccable-*.md text eol=lf
